# =============================================================================
# Qwen3.8-Flash-Next Q4_K_M at 131072 -- ncmoe 0, -ub 2048
# =============================================================================
# A hybrid SSM/attention MoE, and every setting below follows from that shape
# rather than from what the DeepSeek profiles do.
#
#   arch          qwen4exp        176.944 B params, "512x56B", ~6 B active
#   layers        48, full_attention_interval = 4
#                 -> only every 4th layer keeps a KV cache; the other 36 are
#                    SSM/linear (ssm_state 128, ssm_inner 6144, conv kernel 4)
#   experts       512 routed, 10 used, expert FFN 640 -- many tiny experts
#   attention     24 heads / 2 KV heads, head dim 256, sparse indexer top_k 2048
#   extras        hyper-connections (count 4, low rank 320)
#   file          110.957 GiB (5.387 BPW), 3-shard split
#
# NO -mla (deepseek2/4 only), NO --swa-compress (n_swa = 0, no sliding window).
# -fidx does apply -- build_qwen4exp.cpp reads cparams.fused_idx_topk for the
# sparse indexer -- but it is ON BY DEFAULT in this build and has no CLI
# off-switch, so it appears nowhere in the flags (RESULTS 49.8).
#
# THE KV CACHE IS SMALL, and that is the hybrid paying off: 2224 MiB at 131072
# with q8_0, where a dense 48-layer model of this width would want ~12 GiB.
# It leaves the compute buffer as the thing competing with weights for VRAM.
#
# --- MEASURED 2026-09-03, sweep-bench, this box (RTX PRO 6000, 96 GiB) -------
#
# Expert placement, all at -ub 4096 / -c 131072, shallow (N_KV 0) figures:
#
#   -ncmoe   CUDA0 weights   host      pp t/s    tg t/s
#     13        58 160      55 460     2752.8     60.6
#      9        64 560      49 060     2866.0     68.3
#      5        70 960      42 660     2998.5     84.0
#      3        74 460      39 160     3063.3     94.1
#      0        79 710      33 910     3083.1    128.9   <-- shipped (at ub 2048)
#
# Generation is what moves: 60.6 -> 128.9 t/s, a 2.1x swing, while prefill gains
# only 12 %. This model is far more sensitive to resident experts than DeepSeek
# is, because 10 of 512 tiny experts per token means the routing touches a wide,
# thin slice of weights every step -- exactly the traffic PCIe is worst at.
#
# -ncmoe 0 IS NOT "EVERYTHING ON THE GPU", and on this file it happens to land
# well. It places 79 710 MiB on CUDA0 and leaves 33 910 in a plain CPU buffer,
# and that split does not move when -ub shrinks and frees VRAM -- so ~5 GiB of
# card sits unused here and no flag in this file reclaims it (-ot would be the
# lever if it ever matters). --fit --fit-margin 2048 produces the byte-identical
# split, which is why both were tried and neither is better.
#
# DO NOT read that as a safe universal setting: on the Q8_0 file the same
# -ncmoe 0 asks for 126 970 MiB on the card and dies in cudaMalloc before the
# KV cache is even reached. The 79 710 here is where THIS file's tensors happen
# to fall, not a ceiling the loader enforces. See qwen38-flash-next-q8-128k.env,
# which needs -ncmoe 17.
#
# NOTE the buffer type changes with -ncmoe: at 0 the host remainder lands in a
# pageable `CPU buffer`, at >=1 in a pinned `CUDA_Host buffer`. Pinned is the
# faster transfer, yet ncmoe 0 still wins by a wide margin -- 5 GiB more
# resident beats faster streaming of what is left.
#
# --- -ub, at -ncmoe 0 --------------------------------------------------------
#
#   -ub    compute buffer   total VRAM   pp t/s   tg t/s
#   1024        4 522         86 457     3372.4    129.1
#   2048        9 044         90 978     3486.3    128.9   <-- shipped
#   4096       17 064         99 000     does not fit
#
# Generation is flat across -ub (the weights are placed identically), so -ub is
# a pure prefill knob here and 2048 is the largest that fits. 4096 overflows by
# ~2.6 GiB. See the 256k profile for where this forces 1024 instead.
#
# --- depth, since upstream #2404 (2026-09-05, RESULTS 52) --------------------
#
# fe215a8c gathers the cells the sparse indexer selected instead of attending
# over the whole cache, so generation is nearly independent of depth. Nothing
# in this file changes -- the placement above is what it always was -- but the
# curve does (500 W cap, sweep-bench, before -> after):
#
#   N_KV        pp             tg
#   0        3105 -> 3106    129.4 -> 129.7
#   32 768   2725 -> 2716     92.1 -> 103.8
#   96 256   1638 -> 1633     62.2 ->  87.8
#   129 024  1008 -> 1001     52.9 ->  81.3
#
# Prefill is untouched (the change is in the single-token graph only) and the
# gather does not engage below 3 x top_k = 6144 tokens, so short prompts are
# bit-for-bit the old path.
#
#   ./serve.sh qwen38-flash-next-q4km-128k
# -----------------------------------------------------------------------------

: "${IK_MODEL_GLOB:=${IK_MODELS_ROOT}/lmstudio-community/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-GGUF/Qwen3.8-Flash-Next-Q4_K_M-00001-of-*.gguf}"

: "${IK_ALIAS:=qwen38-flash-next-q4km-128k}"
: "${IK_CTX:=131072}"

# Experts compute on the GPU: -rtr would repack the host remainder into a type
# with no CUDA kernel and pin that GEMM to the CPU (RESULTS 21).
: "${IK_RTR:=0}"

# 0 = put as much on the card as the loader will take. See the table above.
: "${IK_FIT:=0}"
: "${IK_NCMOE:=0}"

# Largest micro-batch that fits at ncmoe 0. Raising this to 4096 costs 8 GiB of
# compute buffer and will not load.
: "${IK_UBATCH:=2048}"
: "${IK_BATCH:=2048}"

# Ordinary GQA cache on the 12 full-attention layers, not a latent: q8_0 is
# sound here and halves it. f16 is the fallback if quality ever regresses.
: "${IK_CTK:=q8_0}"
: "${IK_CTV:=q8_0}"

: "${IK_THREADS:=8}"
: "${IK_THREADS_BATCH:=24}"

# NOTE: -thp is on by default in this toolkit and the log shows
# "mmap with huge page size 2 MiB failed (Cannot allocate memory)" for this
# model -- the host remainder does not get huge pages. Harmless, but it means
# the -thp win measured for DeepSeek does not apply here. Unmeasured whether
# raising vm.nr_hugepages would help.
